<h2>A record you can learn from</h2><p>A useful trading journal keeps the reasoning behind a trade alongside its outcome. Record the setup, intended invalidation point and what actually happened during execution.</p><h2>Review the process</h2><p>Use consistent labels for setups and mistakes so that sessions can be compared. Separate a planned loss from a decision that ignored the plan. Both may look the same in an account statement, but they require different responses.</p><h2>Keep a weekly review</h2><p>Look for repeated deviations and choose a concrete adjustment for the next week. Keep that adjustment small enough to assess in the following review.</p><p>This article belongs to the product archive. Check <a href="https://www.fynxfinanceworld.com">FYNX Finance World</a> for the current journal experience and feature availability.</p>